35
00:01:08.638 --> 00:01:10.200
My name is Luciano, and today I'm joined by

36
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.822
Eoin, and this is AWS Bites.

37
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.894
OK, so just before we get into the meat of

38
00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:25.856
the topic of today, I just want to recap quickly

39
00:01:26.056 --> 00:01:29.318
how important it is that your containers start

40
00:01:29.418 --> 00:01:29.718
fast.

41
00:01:30.318 --> 00:01:32.339
And there are a few reasons, but the main one

42
00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:35.101
to me is that if you are dealing with solutions

43
00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.882
that can have traffic spikes, and you built it

44
00:01:37.922 --> 00:01:40.383
in such a way that new infrastructure can come

45
00:01:40.464 --> 00:01:42.304
up to deal with the spike, and this

46
00:01:42.324 --> 00:01:43.705
infrastructure is using containers,

47
00:01:43.845 --> 00:01:44.245
guess what?

48
00:01:44.426 --> 00:01:46.707
If your containers are very slow at starting,

49
00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:49.029
then you're not going to be so efficient at

50
00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:50.611
dealing with the spike because it's going to

51
00:01:50.631 --> 00:01:52.573
take some time before those new containers are

52
00:01:52.613 --> 00:01:55.417
actually going to start to handle requests.

53
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:59.161
So you want your containers to start fast to be

54
00:01:59.201 --> 00:02:00.302
able to deal with spikes.

55
00:02:00.823 --> 00:02:04.167
And similarly, if you're doing quick or you're

56
00:02:04.187 --> 00:02:05.428
doing frequent deployments,

57
00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:08.591
and maybe you have an outage and you figure out

58
00:02:08.611 --> 00:02:09.992
there is a bug and you have a fix and

59
00:02:10.012 --> 00:02:11.714
you want to ship it as quick as possible.

60
00:02:12.054 --> 00:02:13.695
If you are doing all of that through containers,

61
00:02:13.755 --> 00:02:15.837
of course you want your new containers,

62
00:02:15.877 --> 00:02:17.559
the one that fixes the issue, to come up as

63
00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:19.580
quick as possible because that means restoring

64
00:02:19.881 --> 00:02:21.022
your environment quickly.

65
00:02:21.782 --> 00:02:23.864
And similarly, if you run short-lived jobs,

66
00:02:24.764 --> 00:02:26.185
of course, you don't want to spend most of the

67
00:02:26.225 --> 00:02:28.167
time just bootstrapping a container that then

68
00:02:28.227 --> 00:02:29.027
finishes very quickly.

69
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:30.729
So it's important to balance it out.

70
00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:32.410
This is very common when you have,

71
00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.411
I don't know, MapReduce jobs, when you are

72
00:02:34.491 --> 00:02:37.053
distributing lots of computation to many

73
00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:38.915
containers, and they may be not running for a

74
00:02:38.955 --> 00:02:41.136
long time, just giving you that scale of

75
00:02:41.196 --> 00:02:42.297
parallelizing the workload.

76
00:02:42.677 --> 00:02:45.099
You want, of course, each container to come up

77
00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:46.940
quickly and start to do the job as quick as

78
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:47.321
possible.

79
00:02:48.202 --> 00:02:50.343
then when you have, in general, user-facing

80
00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:52.885
latency, of course, the startup container time

81
00:02:53.085 --> 00:02:54.125
is in the critical path.

82
00:02:54.586 --> 00:02:57.387
So we want to minimize the time between needing

83
00:02:57.447 --> 00:03:00.309
to compute and when the compute actually starts

84
00:03:00.729 --> 00:03:01.970
and does useful work.

85
00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.031
So with all of that being said,

86
00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:06.372
and I hope that gives us a good context,

87
00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:09.434
what are some of the more common advices that we

88
00:03:09.454 --> 00:03:10.315
can leverage to

89
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.271
make our kind of dummy container.

90
00:03:13.531 --> 00:03:15.392
We didn't think too much about it and optimize

91
00:03:15.432 --> 00:03:16.092
it a little bit.

92
00:03:16.132 --> 00:03:17.313
So it starts faster.

93
00:03:17.513 --> 00:03:20.374
Well, the simple stuff is don't put things in

94
00:03:20.394 --> 00:03:22.415
your images you don't need because smaller

95
00:03:22.475 --> 00:03:24.516
images means you have less data to download and

96
00:03:24.536 --> 00:03:27.198
decompress, less IO and generally faster

97
00:03:27.218 --> 00:03:27.638
deployments.

98
00:03:28.258 --> 00:03:31.620
So that means using .dockerignore files to

99
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.341
exclude files from your build context,

100
00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:36.702
which would then be picked up by copy commands

101
00:03:36.742 --> 00:03:37.663
within your Docker file.

102
00:03:38.541 --> 00:03:40.543
Also removing package manager caches and

103
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:41.304
temporary files.

104
00:03:42.144 --> 00:03:44.607
Deleting files in a later Docker layer with a

105
00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:46.809
separate command won't make an earlier layer

106
00:03:46.829 --> 00:03:48.911
smaller, so you need to do it all in the

107
00:03:48.971 --> 00:03:49.712
one run instruction.

108
00:03:50.552 --> 00:03:53.755
And Luciano actually was inspired to write a

109
00:03:53.796 --> 00:03:55.497
deep dive into Docker layers, which I really

110
00:03:55.737 --> 00:03:57.379
recommend reading.

111
00:03:57.957 --> 00:04:00.117
It emerged from all the work you did on

112
00:04:00.338 --> 00:04:01.538
researching this episode.

113
00:04:01.558 --> 00:04:02.818
So a link to that will be in the show

114
00:04:02.878 --> 00:04:03.018
notes.

115
00:04:03.758 --> 00:04:06.219
Another piece of advice is using multi-stage

116
00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:06.519
builds.

117
00:04:06.539 --> 00:04:07.319
That's quite common now.

118
00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:08.079
You see it quite a lot.

119
00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:10.060
So you can compile your application with all the

120
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.460
necessary tools, then have a slimmer base image

121
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.101
and copy all of the runtime artifacts into

122
00:04:15.181 --> 00:04:16.701
something clean and lean.

123
00:04:18.201 --> 00:04:20.522
Then using leaner base images with like Slim or

124
00:04:20.582 --> 00:04:22.642
Distroless or even Scratch where appropriate.

125
00:04:23.002 --> 00:04:25.683
Just remember you can make debugging harder if

126
00:04:25.703 --> 00:04:26.883
you need to shell into a container.

127
00:04:27.414 --> 00:04:30.215
and open up Vim or even run things like PS.

128
00:04:32.235 --> 00:04:33.616
Structure layers for caching as well.

129
00:04:33.656 --> 00:04:35.576
So you can put infrequently changing

130
00:04:35.596 --> 00:04:37.877
dependencies first before your application code.

131
00:04:38.457 --> 00:04:39.857
And you can also use things like compiled

132
00:04:39.897 --> 00:04:42.798
languages, you know, Go, C, C++, Rust,

133
00:04:42.858 --> 00:04:43.118
Zig.

134
00:04:43.438 --> 00:04:46.119
They can help you produce very small runtime

135
00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:46.499
images.

136
00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:49.400
Sometimes it could just be a little single

137
00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:49.700
binary.

138
00:04:50.678 --> 00:04:52.779
We won't spend the whole episode talking about

139
00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:53.780
Dockerfile optimization.

140
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.702
That's a little bit standard, I guess.

141
00:04:56.722 --> 00:04:58.443
There's loads of resources out there for that.

142
00:04:58.983 --> 00:05:01.044
But by the way, there are other things you could

143
00:05:01.064 --> 00:05:03.386
do depending on which technology you're using to

144
00:05:03.406 --> 00:05:04.006
run your services.

145
00:05:04.366 --> 00:05:05.527
Like if you're using ECS on EC2,

146
00:05:06.369 --> 00:05:07.890
you can use bridge networking mode,

147
00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.933
and that cuts out the time to provision the ENI

148
00:05:10.993 --> 00:05:11.593
for each task.

149
00:05:12.013 --> 00:05:13.555
And that's something that could really add a lot

150
00:05:13.715 --> 00:05:14.916
of time to your launch.

151
00:05:15.616 --> 00:05:17.277
The trade-off is that you would lose the ability

152
00:05:17.297 --> 00:05:19.519
to have dedicated security groups for your

153
00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:19.859
tasks.

154
00:05:19.959 --> 00:05:21.781
You have to use the EC2's security group.

155
00:05:22.461 --> 00:05:24.723
But that's an aside, that depends where you're

156
00:05:24.743 --> 00:05:25.204
deploying to.

157
00:05:26.605 --> 00:05:28.626
Let's get into, I suppose, some more of the

158
00:05:28.666 --> 00:05:31.989
deployment and startup time optimizations,

159
00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:33.410
starting with compression.

160
00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.933
Yes, compression, it's an interesting topic

161
00:05:36.013 --> 00:05:37.535
because, again, it's one of those things that I

162
00:05:37.575 --> 00:05:39.297
don't feel like many people are actually

163
00:05:39.457 --> 00:05:41.039
exploring or leveraging enough.

164
00:05:41.439 --> 00:05:43.502
So let's talk about it and let's see what's

165
00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:43.962
possible.

166
00:05:44.703 --> 00:05:46.385
And first of all, I think it's important to

167
00:05:46.405 --> 00:05:49.228
understand that a container image is effectively

168
00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:53.213
It's made up of layers and layers are typically

169
00:05:53.253 --> 00:05:56.577
stored as compressed archives in a registry and

170
00:05:56.717 --> 00:05:59.400
most often it's using something like gzip for

171
00:05:59.461 --> 00:05:59.961
compression.

172
00:06:00.442 --> 00:06:01.883
And when we talk about compression we always

173
00:06:01.924 --> 00:06:03.606
need to remember what are the trade-offs there.

174
00:06:03.966 --> 00:06:05.648
We are effectively trying to reduce the number

175
00:06:05.668 --> 00:06:07.490
of bytes that we need to download because

176
00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:08.672
compression effectively

177
00:06:09.152 --> 00:06:11.054
minimizes the amount of bytes that that's what's

178
00:06:11.154 --> 00:06:11.554
built for.

179
00:06:11.955 --> 00:06:13.376
But at the same time, to be able to use

180
00:06:13.416 --> 00:06:15.898
the information, so in this case, the files

181
00:06:15.978 --> 00:06:18.440
within a Docker image, you need to decompress it

182
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:18.741
first.

183
00:06:18.841 --> 00:06:21.603
So effectively, we are saving time at download

184
00:06:21.743 --> 00:06:24.306
because we are downloading less, but then we

185
00:06:24.346 --> 00:06:26.668
might be spending more time to actually make

186
00:06:26.768 --> 00:06:28.729
that download useful because we'll need to

187
00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:30.171
decompress and that's all CPU

188
00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:31.451
time that it spent.

189
00:06:31.832 --> 00:06:33.552
So it's important to figure out what is the

190
00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:35.293
right balance, and different compression

191
00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:36.954
algorithms can also be tuned.

192
00:06:36.974 --> 00:06:38.454
They have different compression levels,

193
00:06:38.494 --> 00:06:40.835
for example, where you can say, OK,

194
00:06:40.875 --> 00:06:42.856
compress more, but that generally means that

195
00:06:42.876 --> 00:06:45.677
then you will need more CPU work to decompress,

196
00:06:46.157 --> 00:06:48.378
or compress less, which means you save less

197
00:06:48.418 --> 00:06:50.039
bytes, but then it's much faster at

198
00:06:50.059 --> 00:06:50.579
decompressing.

199
00:06:50.939 --> 00:06:52.701
And again, depending on the algorithm,

200
00:06:52.901 --> 00:06:54.522
there are lots of nuances there.

201
00:06:54.663 --> 00:06:56.664
So it's not an obvious choice, like which

202
00:06:56.724 --> 00:06:58.706
algorithm will be the best for your specific use

203
00:06:58.746 --> 00:07:00.708
case, or which compression level will be the

204
00:07:00.768 --> 00:07:00.988
best.

205
00:07:01.609 --> 00:07:03.671
But when it comes to containers, we have two

206
00:07:03.751 --> 00:07:04.071
options.

207
00:07:04.211 --> 00:07:05.873
There is gzip, which we already mentioned,

208
00:07:05.893 --> 00:07:09.716
but there is also zstd, also called zstandard.

209
00:07:10.761 --> 00:07:13.382
which is supported by the OCI standard.

210
00:07:13.942 --> 00:07:17.303
So when you compare ZSTD with gzip,

211
00:07:18.143 --> 00:07:21.144
generally ZSTD has very good compression rates,

212
00:07:21.284 --> 00:07:23.665
but also significantly faster decompression.

213
00:07:23.725 --> 00:07:26.025
I think that was one of the design goals of

214
00:07:26.126 --> 00:07:27.466
ZSTD when it was created.

215
00:07:27.866 --> 00:07:30.027
How can we achieve competitive levels of

216
00:07:30.087 --> 00:07:32.187
compression without burdening too much on the

217
00:07:32.227 --> 00:07:33.408
CPU for decompression?

218
00:07:33.988 --> 00:07:35.828
And AWS has done their own research,

219
00:07:36.068 --> 00:07:37.909
and you can probably find this somewhere in the

220
00:07:37.989 --> 00:07:39.829
AWS documentation.

221
00:07:39.869 --> 00:07:41.170
I don't remember exactly where we found it,

222
00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:44.471
but we found that they claim that if you use

223
00:07:44.691 --> 00:07:48.152
ZSTD, you get up to 27% faster Fargate startup

224
00:07:48.192 --> 00:07:50.693
times just by switching from gzip to ZSTD.

225
00:07:52.593 --> 00:07:54.135
And of course, as I was saying,

226
00:07:54.195 --> 00:07:55.917
there is a tuning there.

227
00:07:56.237 --> 00:07:58.839
So you might use different levels of

228
00:07:58.879 --> 00:07:59.420
compression.

229
00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.903
And AWS suggests that, based on the research,

230
00:08:02.123 --> 00:08:05.046
level 3 is probably a good average that works

231
00:08:05.246 --> 00:08:06.327
well enough in most cases.

232
00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:07.588
So if you don't know where to start,

233
00:08:07.929 --> 00:08:09.810
probably that's a good number to start with.

234
00:08:10.721 --> 00:08:12.663
Now, we're going to be talking more about Soci

235
00:08:13.304 --> 00:08:14.686
and what all of that is.

236
00:08:14.866 --> 00:08:16.127
And I'm just going to mention up front that

237
00:08:16.167 --> 00:08:17.349
there is a little bit of a caveat.

238
00:08:17.449 --> 00:08:21.373
So Soci doesn't support ZSTD, but only gzip.

239
00:08:21.573 --> 00:08:24.216
So I suppose that that's one of the things we'll

240
00:08:24.236 --> 00:08:26.299
need to keep in mind when we decide exactly

241
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.062
which kind of optimizations we can use to make

242
00:08:29.122 --> 00:08:30.623
our container start fast.

243
00:08:31.534 --> 00:08:33.476
So I'm just going to give you a little bit

244
00:08:33.496 --> 00:08:35.058
of a spoiler on Soci, and then I'm going to

245
00:08:35.098 --> 00:08:37.160
let Eoin introduce all of that.

246
00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:40.723
But the main idea is that we are used to

247
00:08:40.743 --> 00:08:43.346
thinking about containers as it's a package that

248
00:08:43.386 --> 00:08:44.527
gives you everything you need to run an

249
00:08:44.567 --> 00:08:45.108
application.

250
00:08:45.388 --> 00:08:46.869
So in order to start that application,

251
00:08:46.889 --> 00:08:49.412
you need to download everything, somehow create

252
00:08:49.452 --> 00:08:51.234
kind of a file system that has all these files

253
00:08:51.274 --> 00:08:53.136
available, and then you can start up an isolated

254
00:08:53.196 --> 00:08:54.937
process with all of this information.

255
00:08:55.618 --> 00:08:57.880
The idea of Soci is a little bit different in

256
00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.664
the sense that it is trying to effectively start

257
00:09:01.684 --> 00:09:02.986
the container as quick as possible.

258
00:09:03.006 --> 00:09:05.228
So the idea is like, can we start it even

259
00:09:05.288 --> 00:09:07.390
before we have made all the files available?

260
00:09:07.891 --> 00:09:10.393
Then the files can be loaded lazily as needed,

261
00:09:10.974 --> 00:09:13.156
but the startup of that process can be much

262
00:09:13.196 --> 00:09:13.536
faster.

263
00:09:13.556 --> 00:09:14.938
So that's kind of the general idea.

264
00:09:15.258 --> 00:09:17.060
What else can we share about Soci?

265
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.841
So Soci is all about becoming faster by having

266
00:09:20.881 --> 00:09:22.642
this seekable OCI image.

267
00:09:23.002 --> 00:09:24.783
AWS developed it and open sourced it,

268
00:09:24.983 --> 00:09:26.383
and it's built around Containerd.

269
00:09:26.783 --> 00:09:28.464
It's been around for quite a few years,

270
00:09:28.824 --> 00:09:31.545
but it seems like it's still relatively unknown

271
00:09:31.645 --> 00:09:32.685
and underutilized.

272
00:09:33.586 --> 00:09:34.746
That's why we wanted to talk about it.

273
00:09:35.026 --> 00:09:38.507
So normal container startup works by having your

274
00:09:38.727 --> 00:09:40.028
images with multiple layers.

275
00:09:40.588 --> 00:09:43.189
Each of them has set of file system changes in

276
00:09:43.209 --> 00:09:43.249
it.

277
00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:46.302
And they're stored in compressed archives in a

278
00:09:46.342 --> 00:09:47.422
registry like ECR.

279
00:09:47.462 --> 00:09:48.783
That's normally how it's done.

280
00:09:49.323 --> 00:09:52.205
When you start a container, a runtime like

281
00:09:52.245 --> 00:09:56.147
Fargate or the ECS agent on EC2 will have to

282
00:09:56.187 --> 00:09:58.169
download the required image layers from the

283
00:09:58.209 --> 00:09:58.669
repository.

284
00:09:59.109 --> 00:10:01.150
That could be ECR, which is backed by S3.

285
00:10:02.475 --> 00:10:04.717
Then it would decompress and unpack them locally

286
00:10:05.098 --> 00:10:07.220
and assemble a file system, typically using an

287
00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:08.041
overlay FS.

288
00:10:08.742 --> 00:10:10.263
After that, it can go about the process of

289
00:10:10.303 --> 00:10:11.064
starting the container.

290
00:10:11.425 --> 00:10:13.487
And once the container starts, the application

291
00:10:13.527 --> 00:10:15.229
can access the files in the image normally.

292
00:10:15.869 --> 00:10:17.191
The problem is that all the downloading and

293
00:10:17.251 --> 00:10:19.113
unpacking happens up front, even if the

294
00:10:19.133 --> 00:10:21.496
application only needs a small percentage of

295
00:10:21.516 --> 00:10:22.777
those files immediately or at all.

296
00:10:23.329 --> 00:10:24.970
So if you imagine a five gigabyte container

297
00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.770
image with a large application and hundreds of

298
00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:29.371
dependencies and maybe assets to go along with

299
00:10:29.411 --> 00:10:31.928
it, the application might only need 100 or 200

300
00:10:31.972 --> 00:10:33.452
megabytes of those files during startup,

301
00:10:33.692 --> 00:10:35.213
but we're still downloading and unpacking

302
00:10:35.313 --> 00:10:36.113
everything upfront.

303
00:10:36.613 --> 00:10:38.674
And this is the opportunity for optimization.

304
00:10:38.814 --> 00:10:40.055
So what changes with Soci?

305
00:10:40.595 --> 00:10:42.435
Yeah, Soci introduces a different kind of

306
00:10:42.495 --> 00:10:44.916
snapshotter, which is the component responsible

307
00:10:44.956 --> 00:10:46.777
for preparing the container file system.

308
00:10:47.257 --> 00:10:48.757
So as you said, instead of downloading

309
00:10:49.038 --> 00:10:49.838
everything upfront,

310
00:10:50.358 --> 00:10:52.201
the SOCI snapshot works a little bit

311
00:10:52.241 --> 00:10:52.721
differently.

312
00:10:53.202 --> 00:10:55.165
It just downloads a small index.

313
00:10:55.285 --> 00:10:56.847
So effectively, there is this new piece of

314
00:10:56.907 --> 00:10:57.348
information.

315
00:10:57.368 --> 00:10:59.631
It's an index that describes which files exist

316
00:10:59.791 --> 00:11:01.894
and where they are located within the set of

317
00:11:01.954 --> 00:11:04.537
layers that the container image has available.

318
00:11:05.379 --> 00:11:06.680
Then it creates a file system.

319
00:11:07.461 --> 00:11:09.784
that is effectively like a virtual file system

320
00:11:09.864 --> 00:11:12.588
using FUSE, the file system in user space.

321
00:11:13.309 --> 00:11:16.012
And it doesn't need to download all the layers

322
00:11:16.213 --> 00:11:17.634
to be able to do that because it can use

323
00:11:17.654 --> 00:11:20.158
the index to understand which files are going to

324
00:11:20.178 --> 00:11:21.900
be available and what is the structure of these

325
00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:22.220
files.

326
00:11:23.021 --> 00:11:24.701
At that point, it can start the container with

327
00:11:24.721 --> 00:11:26.402
the expected file system available.

328
00:11:27.242 --> 00:11:29.182
And it fetches only the relevant portion of the

329
00:11:29.242 --> 00:11:31.323
image as the application starts to access the

330
00:11:31.343 --> 00:11:31.603
file.

331
00:11:31.663 --> 00:11:33.924
So it makes this virtual file system available

332
00:11:33.944 --> 00:11:34.624
to the application.

333
00:11:34.744 --> 00:11:37.024
And the application will ask for the specific

334
00:11:37.064 --> 00:11:37.344
files.

335
00:11:37.464 --> 00:11:39.905
And those files can be downloaded lazily.

336
00:11:40.285 --> 00:11:42.746
And there is, of course, a cache.

337
00:11:42.846 --> 00:11:44.886
So every time a file has been fetched,

338
00:11:45.086 --> 00:11:46.707
of course, it remains available in the cache.

339
00:11:46.727 --> 00:11:48.887
So it doesn't need to be redownloaded from

340
00:11:48.927 --> 00:11:51.248
scratch or re-unpacked from the container image

341
00:11:51.268 --> 00:11:52.468
if we want to frame it like that.

342
00:11:53.248 --> 00:11:55.328
Now, let's make a little bit of a more concrete

343
00:11:55.449 --> 00:11:55.929
example.

344
00:11:55.949 --> 00:11:57.549
So imagine you have an application,

345
00:11:57.969 --> 00:12:00.189
I don't know, web server written for Node.js.

346
00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:03.470
You might have like an app.js that is kind of

347
00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:05.991
your entry point, so what starts the process,

348
00:12:06.031 --> 00:12:07.811
what brings up the web server and starts to

349
00:12:07.851 --> 00:12:08.631
listen for requests.

350
00:12:09.171 --> 00:12:12.892
So Soci will use the index to figure out where

351
00:12:12.952 --> 00:12:15.613
is this app.js and just make it available so it

352
00:12:15.633 --> 00:12:17.313
can be launched and the process starts.

353
00:12:17.993 --> 00:12:19.474
Then, of course, you can imagine, I don't know,

354
00:12:19.514 --> 00:12:22.455
maybe this web application has some assets like

355
00:12:22.495 --> 00:12:24.096
CSS or images or whatever.

356
00:12:24.397 --> 00:12:26.097
As the users will start to send requests,

357
00:12:26.678 --> 00:12:28.959
the first time that it's needing specific files,

358
00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:31.700
those files will be loaded lazily and then

359
00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:32.141
cached.

360
00:12:32.501 --> 00:12:34.702
And the next time, they are going to be readily

361
00:12:34.802 --> 00:12:35.202
available.

362
00:12:35.963 --> 00:12:39.044
So the idea is that you are starting much faster

363
00:12:39.164 --> 00:12:39.485
and then

364
00:12:40.478 --> 00:12:42.219
getting the files might be a little bit slower

365
00:12:42.239 --> 00:12:43.899
because they are not immediately available and

366
00:12:43.919 --> 00:12:45.280
they need to be fetched on demand.

367
00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:47.861
So we can call this idea effectively lazy

368
00:12:47.881 --> 00:12:48.961
loading for containers.

369
00:12:49.762 --> 00:12:51.242
Now, why it is particularly clever?

370
00:12:51.442 --> 00:12:53.783
I think the design is really interesting because

371
00:12:54.224 --> 00:12:57.565
it tries not to change what a Docker container

372
00:12:57.605 --> 00:12:58.405
image looks like.

373
00:12:58.665 --> 00:13:00.846
It just adds additional metadata on top.

374
00:13:01.407 --> 00:13:03.848
So you can just take an existing Docker image

375
00:13:03.888 --> 00:13:04.688
that you have already

376
00:13:05.068 --> 00:13:06.529
And you don't need to change anything on it.

377
00:13:06.549 --> 00:13:08.790
You just need to follow a process that allows

378
00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:10.651
you to build the SOCI index.

379
00:13:10.731 --> 00:13:13.912
So that allows you to incrementally adopt this

380
00:13:13.972 --> 00:13:14.932
idea, this technology.

381
00:13:15.633 --> 00:13:17.373
And all of that is transparent to the

382
00:13:17.413 --> 00:13:17.874
application.

383
00:13:17.994 --> 00:13:19.995
You don't need to do any application change in

384
00:13:20.035 --> 00:13:22.976
order to support this particular approach of

385
00:13:23.116 --> 00:13:25.117
loading and starting container images.

386
00:13:25.497 --> 00:13:27.217
Also, interesting enough, there is native

387
00:13:27.478 --> 00:13:28.418
Fargate integration.

388
00:13:28.438 --> 00:13:31.208
So if you're running containers on Fargate,

389
00:13:31.208 --> 00:13:32.799
Soci automatically works.

390
00:13:32.879 --> 00:13:35.559
Of course, you need to publish your images in a

391
00:13:35.639 --> 00:13:37.440
way that you are also building and publishing

392
00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:38.440
this SOCI index.

393
00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.141
But otherwise, you don't have to enable anything

394
00:13:41.181 --> 00:13:43.661
particular or install anything in particular at

395
00:13:43.741 --> 00:13:45.762
the Fargate level or at the application level.

396
00:13:46.432 --> 00:13:47.933
And it's important to mention that there are

397
00:13:47.973 --> 00:13:48.393
trade-offs.

398
00:13:48.773 --> 00:13:51.034
It's not solving all the problems and making

399
00:13:51.114 --> 00:13:52.355
everything magically faster.

400
00:13:52.615 --> 00:13:54.155
So it's important to understand what is the

401
00:13:54.175 --> 00:13:54.516
trade-off.

402
00:13:54.576 --> 00:13:56.957
The trade-off is starting that process as quick

403
00:13:56.977 --> 00:13:57.517
as possible.

404
00:13:57.997 --> 00:13:59.578
So, for example, you can start to send requests,

405
00:14:00.098 --> 00:14:01.719
but then, of course, you might be a little bit

406
00:14:01.739 --> 00:14:04.200
slower in serving the initial first request

407
00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.441
because the lazy loading is going to take some

408
00:14:06.481 --> 00:14:06.761
time.

409
00:14:06.881 --> 00:14:08.922
It's going to be decompressing on the fly,

410
00:14:09.202 --> 00:14:11.223
for example, the first time it's loading a new

411
00:14:11.243 --> 00:14:11.443
file.

412
00:14:12.023 --> 00:14:14.126
So I suppose the trade-off is that,

413
00:14:14.566 --> 00:14:16.488
yes, you're starting faster, but your initial

414
00:14:16.508 --> 00:14:17.850
request might be a little bit slower.

415
00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:20.313
It's difficult to tell exactly what is the

416
00:14:20.373 --> 00:14:22.055
impact of that because it's very application

417
00:14:22.075 --> 00:14:22.555
specific.

418
00:14:23.016 --> 00:14:25.338
If you have very few files, large files,

419
00:14:25.599 --> 00:14:28.122
lots of files, small files, different kinds of

420
00:14:28.162 --> 00:14:30.184
files that compress differently, you might have

421
00:14:30.244 --> 00:14:32.426
very different performance characteristics.

422
00:14:33.167 --> 00:14:38.146
AWS gives us a rough idea that around 250

423
00:14:38.232 --> 00:14:42.296
megabytes of compressed image, that's where you

424
00:14:42.316 --> 00:14:44.659
start to see more benefits than downsides.

425
00:14:44.999 --> 00:14:47.121
So that's probably a good threshold to start to

426
00:14:47.181 --> 00:14:48.543
explore when you might want to.

427
00:14:48.883 --> 00:14:50.965
experiment with such and see if it's beneficial

428
00:14:51.005 --> 00:14:51.645
to you or not.

429
00:14:52.206 --> 00:14:53.827
If you have smaller images, probably it's not

430
00:14:53.927 --> 00:14:56.850
worth it, because I think that the extra cost of

431
00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:01.314
all this machinery is not justified for a

432
00:15:01.334 --> 00:15:02.035
smaller image.

433
00:15:02.095 --> 00:15:03.676
You're probably not going to see benefits,

434
00:15:03.736 --> 00:15:04.897
and you're probably just going to see more of

435
00:15:04.917 --> 00:15:06.639
the downsides rather than the benefits.

436
00:15:07.219 --> 00:15:09.420
So again, the key takeaway is that it's not

437
00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:11.441
something you should just enable all the time by

438
00:15:11.461 --> 00:15:11.842
default.

439
00:15:12.022 --> 00:15:13.603
It's not always going to make things faster.

440
00:15:14.163 --> 00:15:15.484
So try to understand the trade-offs,

441
00:15:15.824 --> 00:15:17.865
do your own tests, and see where those

442
00:15:17.885 --> 00:15:19.566
trade-offs are beneficial to you or not.

443
00:15:20.186 --> 00:15:22.188
Now, I already mentioned we can use Soci on

444
00:15:22.228 --> 00:15:22.708
Fargate.

445
00:15:22.888 --> 00:15:25.329
What else can we do, Eoin, on AWS?

446
00:15:25.569 --> 00:15:28.731
Yeah, we mentioned Fargate, and we're going to

447
00:15:28.871 --> 00:15:29.912
talk about how you

448
00:15:30.465 --> 00:15:31.105
it all up and running.

449
00:15:31.225 --> 00:15:33.387
But basically, once you have the metadata there,

450
00:15:33.447 --> 00:15:34.387
it'll work on Fargate.

451
00:15:34.527 --> 00:15:35.248
So we'll go through that.

452
00:15:35.288 --> 00:15:36.688
And we'll also go through our benchmarking

453
00:15:36.708 --> 00:15:38.749
results in a bit, which is interesting.

454
00:15:38.989 --> 00:15:41.571
In AWS, the other services where you could use

455
00:15:41.631 --> 00:15:43.912
it are SageMaker, so SageMaker Studio and

456
00:15:43.952 --> 00:15:44.732
Unified Studio.

457
00:15:45.093 --> 00:15:47.574
AWS say that you can get 30% to 70% startup

458
00:15:47.614 --> 00:15:48.214
time reductions.

459
00:15:49.555 --> 00:15:50.895
EKS is an interesting one.

460
00:15:51.596 --> 00:15:53.977
So Saatchi is a container D snapshotter.

461
00:15:54.037 --> 00:15:55.718
So it can be deployed wherever you control

462
00:15:56.374 --> 00:15:57.274
the container runtime.

463
00:15:57.995 --> 00:16:00.736
AWS has also introduced something called Soci

464
00:16:00.796 --> 00:16:02.697
Parallel Pull, which works with EKS and

465
00:16:02.817 --> 00:16:05.178
optimizes concurrent layer downloading.

466
00:16:05.518 --> 00:16:07.419
So it's the same tooling, the same ecosystem,

467
00:16:07.799 --> 00:16:09.600
but it's a different optimization mechanism.

468
00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:12.681
So on EKS, you can then benefit from pulling

469
00:16:13.361 --> 00:16:16.143
parts of the image in separate parallel threads,

470
00:16:16.203 --> 00:16:16.543
if you like.

471
00:16:17.103 --> 00:16:18.843
Now, ECS on EC2 is a funny one.

472
00:16:19.484 --> 00:16:21.704
Unfortunately, there's no native Soci support.

473
00:16:22.344 --> 00:16:23.465
It's still not available there.

474
00:16:23.965 --> 00:16:26.845
There's been a longstanding AWS roadmap request

475
00:16:27.045 --> 00:16:28.106
for Soci integration.

476
00:16:29.046 --> 00:16:32.407
And it depends on supporting containerd in ECS

477
00:16:32.467 --> 00:16:36.108
and the ECS agent that drives ECS on EC2.

478
00:16:36.788 --> 00:16:38.368
It's quite old and based on Docker,

479
00:16:38.648 --> 00:16:40.809
and there doesn't seem to be a lot of movement

480
00:16:40.829 --> 00:16:41.869
around evolving it.

481
00:16:42.849 --> 00:16:43.850
We'll have to watch that space.

482
00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:45.510
So for today,

483
00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.421
Fargate is really the obvious place to start.

484
00:16:48.801 --> 00:16:50.702
So before we go into our experiences,

485
00:16:51.042 --> 00:16:53.043
everything we built, how does Soci work,

486
00:16:53.063 --> 00:16:53.363
Luciano?

487
00:16:53.383 --> 00:16:54.284
Do you want to break it down?

488
00:16:55.064 --> 00:16:57.385
Yes, I think we already mentioned the high-level

489
00:16:57.405 --> 00:16:57.706
idea.

490
00:16:57.746 --> 00:16:59.907
So let's deep dive a little bit more.

491
00:17:00.527 --> 00:17:04.549
So the idea is that if we take a closer

492
00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:06.470
look at container layers, we basically need to

493
00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.451
think about a container layer as an immutable

494
00:17:08.531 --> 00:17:10.011
set of file system changes.

495
00:17:10.472 --> 00:17:12.212
If you have in mind, for example,

496
00:17:12.252 --> 00:17:12.933
a Docker file,

497
00:17:13.593 --> 00:17:15.795
is defined, you know, you're kind of running

498
00:17:16.215 --> 00:17:17.996
different, let's call them instructions.

499
00:17:18.496 --> 00:17:20.718
And then each step is effectively building a

500
00:17:20.778 --> 00:17:22.979
layer that will become part of the final

501
00:17:23.039 --> 00:17:23.720
container image.

502
00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.002
So, and those layers are kind of stacked

503
00:17:26.122 --> 00:17:26.542
together.

504
00:17:26.562 --> 00:17:30.104
And if you basically read all of them in

505
00:17:30.144 --> 00:17:32.086
sequence, you are effectively building a file

506
00:17:32.126 --> 00:17:32.426
system.

507
00:17:32.966 --> 00:17:34.427
The idea of each layer is that they are

508
00:17:34.467 --> 00:17:37.589
reusable, cacheable, and stored as a compressed

509
00:17:37.829 --> 00:17:38.249
archive.

510
00:17:38.589 --> 00:17:41.090
So it's effectively like a tar of all the files

511
00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:42.491
in that layer that can be compressed,

512
00:17:42.531 --> 00:17:44.052
and then you're kind of stacking up all these

513
00:17:44.112 --> 00:17:46.393
layers to accumulate file system changes.

514
00:17:46.813 --> 00:17:49.875
So again, rolling them back from the first layer

515
00:17:49.895 --> 00:17:52.176
to the last gives you the file system that you

516
00:17:52.196 --> 00:17:53.357
can use for your application.

517
00:17:54.134 --> 00:17:58.360
Now imagine that one of these layers is called

518
00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.183
layer.tar.gz.

519
00:18:00.663 --> 00:18:01.625
What is the problem here?

520
00:18:02.065 --> 00:18:04.269
We want to build an index that tells us,

521
00:18:04.749 --> 00:18:06.932
if you ever need that file app.js,

522
00:18:06.972 --> 00:18:09.716
for example, where can I find this file among

523
00:18:09.857 --> 00:18:10.477
all these layers?

524
00:18:11.098 --> 00:18:13.081
And of course, you want the last version of that

525
00:18:13.141 --> 00:18:14.803
file, because of course, you might have a layer

526
00:18:14.844 --> 00:18:16.926
that replaces a file at some point.

527
00:18:17.227 --> 00:18:19.150
So you need to have an index that tells you

528
00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:22.915
exactly how do you load efficiently only app.js

529
00:18:22.975 --> 00:18:25.719
and not having to unpack all the layers and

530
00:18:25.779 --> 00:18:27.281
rebuild the entire file system from scratch.

531
00:18:28.082 --> 00:18:30.045
And it's not as simple as it might sound when

532
00:18:30.085 --> 00:18:32.128
you bring compression into the mix,

533
00:18:32.709 --> 00:18:37.236
because you have to use gzip with SOCI.

534
00:18:37.917 --> 00:18:40.160
And if you say, give me that app.js,

535
00:18:40.942 --> 00:18:43.085
it's somewhere in a compressed form.

536
00:18:43.485 --> 00:18:45.106
in the middle of a layer.

537
00:18:45.567 --> 00:18:47.128
So first of all, you need to know which layer,

538
00:18:47.628 --> 00:18:48.709
and then you need to know, okay,

539
00:18:48.809 --> 00:18:51.090
in this layer, there are just a bunch of bytes,

540
00:18:51.591 --> 00:18:54.312
which bytes are relevant to me in a way that

541
00:18:54.352 --> 00:18:56.954
I can decompress those bytes and get the full

542
00:18:57.014 --> 00:19:00.236
file, and potentially not much else that I don't

543
00:19:00.256 --> 00:19:00.677
care about.

544
00:19:01.774 --> 00:19:04.317
And so again, the issue is, if I have to

545
00:19:04.357 --> 00:19:05.798
phrase it differently, it's like you cannot

546
00:19:05.938 --> 00:19:08.881
efficiently jump in a random place,

547
00:19:09.141 --> 00:19:11.083
and I suppose in a gzip stream,

548
00:19:11.624 --> 00:19:13.846
and be reassured, OK, this is exactly where I'm

549
00:19:13.866 --> 00:19:15.087
going to find the file.

550
00:19:15.127 --> 00:19:16.969
You probably need to kind of unwind the entire

551
00:19:17.029 --> 00:19:19.271
stream until you find the specific file you're

552
00:19:19.291 --> 00:19:19.711
looking for.

553
00:19:19.932 --> 00:19:21.593
And this is where Soci does something a little

554
00:19:21.613 --> 00:19:24.436
bit clever, in my opinion, that they call ZTOC,

555
00:19:24.556 --> 00:19:26.177
or Z Table of Contents.

556
00:19:26.798 --> 00:19:27.739
contents, I suppose.

557
00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:32.442
The idea of this table of contents is that when

558
00:19:32.483 --> 00:19:35.085
you create the index, you can effectively

559
00:19:35.505 --> 00:19:37.747
inspect the stream, the gzip stream,

560
00:19:38.347 --> 00:19:41.590
and see exactly which file starts at which byte.

561
00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:44.012
And if you are taking a note of all of

562
00:19:44.072 --> 00:19:46.894
that effectively, like you are bookkeeping and

563
00:19:47.815 --> 00:19:49.476
tracking as, okay, I know that this file is

564
00:19:49.496 --> 00:19:50.397
going to start at this byte,

565
00:19:51.037 --> 00:19:52.918
then the index is effectively what's kind of

566
00:19:53.018 --> 00:19:54.599
solving the problem for you later on,

567
00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:56.421
because you can then look at the index and know

568
00:19:56.481 --> 00:20:00.584
exactly at which byte you can read to get that

569
00:20:00.624 --> 00:20:01.264
particular file.

570
00:20:01.904 --> 00:20:03.946
So again, this is not something that is natively

571
00:20:03.986 --> 00:20:06.127
supported by gzip, and that's why Soci needs to

572
00:20:06.207 --> 00:20:08.589
add this additional structure.

573
00:20:08.749 --> 00:20:10.530
So if gzip had something like a table of

574
00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:12.612
contents, of course, they didn't have to come up

575
00:20:12.632 --> 00:20:13.172
with all of this.

576
00:20:13.232 --> 00:20:15.794
But because it doesn't exist, that's effectively

577
00:20:15.834 --> 00:20:16.975
how they solved the problem.

578
00:20:17.615 --> 00:20:19.255
So once you have something like that,

579
00:20:19.275 --> 00:20:22.356
you can imagine that solving the give me the

580
00:20:22.436 --> 00:20:24.816
app.js problem becomes much simpler,

581
00:20:24.896 --> 00:20:27.817
because Soci can just see, OK, the entry point

582
00:20:27.937 --> 00:20:30.117
is running Node.js with app.js.

583
00:20:30.157 --> 00:20:34.118
So I need to unpack app.js to be able to

584
00:20:34.178 --> 00:20:35.078
start that process.

585
00:20:35.578 --> 00:20:37.498
So it's going to look at, first of all,

586
00:20:37.538 --> 00:20:39.739
which layer contains the last version of that

587
00:20:39.779 --> 00:20:40.059
file.

588
00:20:40.599 --> 00:20:42.339
Then it's going to look at the TOC and figure

589
00:20:42.379 --> 00:20:44.560
out what is the offset in the gzip stream that

590
00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.340
I need to start unpacking for.

591
00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:48.444
Of course there are other additional details

592
00:20:48.504 --> 00:20:50.589
because it's not just the offset but you might

593
00:20:50.649 --> 00:20:52.373
also need to know what is the length that you

594
00:20:52.393 --> 00:20:52.774
need to read.

595
00:20:53.363 --> 00:20:54.584
So it's going to read all of that.

596
00:20:54.924 --> 00:20:57.305
It's going to fetch the information.

597
00:20:57.365 --> 00:21:00.367
And if you deep dive, you might come across the

598
00:21:00.407 --> 00:21:02.088
concept of spans because, of course,

599
00:21:02.468 --> 00:21:08.012
compressed blobs are not covering precise files.

600
00:21:08.132 --> 00:21:08.992
They are kind of blocks.

601
00:21:09.132 --> 00:21:10.613
So you might need to read multiple blocks.

602
00:21:11.153 --> 00:21:12.734
So of course, all of that information is in the

603
00:21:12.774 --> 00:21:13.115
index.

604
00:21:13.155 --> 00:21:14.876
So the index will tell you where to start,

605
00:21:14.936 --> 00:21:16.016
how many blocks you need to read.

606
00:21:16.456 --> 00:21:17.697
Then they can be decompressed.

607
00:21:17.857 --> 00:21:18.898
You have your file available.

608
00:21:19.238 --> 00:21:22.380
So again, interesting idea of Soci,

609
00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.161
they didn't rewrite any technology,

610
00:21:24.181 --> 00:21:25.882
they just layered more stuff on top,

611
00:21:26.042 --> 00:21:28.084
which is a great idea because it makes things

612
00:21:28.264 --> 00:21:31.026
incrementally adoptable without having to change

613
00:21:31.126 --> 00:21:32.406
what's already out there.

614
00:21:33.007 --> 00:21:35.188
And yeah, we'll probably have a link in the show

615
00:21:35.208 --> 00:21:37.890
notes with a deep dive from AWS with more

616
00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.672
details and more in-depth explanations if you're

617
00:21:40.712 --> 00:21:42.513
curious, but I honestly found all this stuff

618
00:21:42.673 --> 00:21:44.734
fascinating and that's why I wanted to share

619
00:21:45.094 --> 00:21:45.875
this particular design.

620
00:21:46.115 --> 00:21:47.696
Now, it's probably a good time to talk about

621
00:21:47.736 --> 00:21:50.898
benchmarks if you really want to know how much

622
00:21:50.938 --> 00:21:52.859
can you save and whether it could be worth to

623
00:21:52.879 --> 00:21:53.419
you or not.

