00:24:42.561 --> 00:24:45.103
and there's also a visualization dashboard with

698
00:24:45.143 --> 00:24:46.665
Plotly for exploring the results.

699
00:24:47.369 --> 00:24:48.269
So what were the results?

700
00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:50.590
Well, some interesting stuff, I think,

701
00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:50.930
in here.

702
00:24:51.051 --> 00:24:52.431
We're not going to go through it in painstaking

703
00:24:52.451 --> 00:24:52.731
detail.

704
00:24:53.231 --> 00:24:55.732
But let's start with our image sizes.

705
00:24:55.752 --> 00:24:58.814
So we had around 70 megabytes compressed for the

706
00:24:58.854 --> 00:25:01.355
small image and 3 gigabytes compressed for the

707
00:25:01.455 --> 00:25:01.875
large image.

708
00:25:02.535 --> 00:25:04.556
So first of all, how does compression affect it?

709
00:25:05.216 --> 00:25:07.878
So looking at gzip versus zstandard versus

710
00:25:07.998 --> 00:25:08.498
uncompressed,

711
00:25:09.489 --> 00:25:11.543
The large image goes from about 6 gigabytes to 3

712
00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:13.491
gigabytes with GZIP, and it's a little bit less

713
00:25:13.531 --> 00:25:15.973
with Z-Standard, like 200 megabytes less.

714
00:25:16.633 --> 00:25:20.155
Small goes from 250 megabytes to about 74 with

715
00:25:20.175 --> 00:25:22.456
GZIP, and 67 with Z-Standard.

716
00:25:23.637 --> 00:25:25.538
So in terms of performance just based on

717
00:25:25.578 --> 00:25:28.880
compression, Zstandard is only about three to

718
00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:32.181
five times faster than gzip on large Fargate

